---
import PublicLayout from "../layouts/PublicLayout.astro";
import AgentSetup from "../components/AgentSetup.astro";
import ProductTour from "../components/ProductTour.astro";
import MemoryCamp from "../components/MemoryCamp.astro";
import Waitlist from "../components/Waitlist.astro";
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---
<PublicLayout title="Lore.AI — Project memory for coding agents" description="Keep project decisions available across coding sessions. Lore manages context and curates reviewable knowledge for the coding agent you already use.">
  <section class="home-hero section" id="problem">
    <div class="hero-copy"><p class="eyebrow">Memory for coding agents</p><h1>Keep project decisions <em>across coding sessions.</em></h1><p class="hero-desc">Pick up yesterday’s work with the decisions behind it. Lore records your coding sessions, keeps useful context available, and turns project knowledge into Markdown you can review in Git.</p>
      <div class="hero-actions"><a class="button button-primary" href={`${base}docs/install/`}>Install Lore <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a><a class="text-link" href={`${base}docs/`}>Read the docs</a></div>
      <p class="compatibility">Works with Claude Code, Codex, OpenCode, Pi, and Hermes.</p><a class="text-link" href="#get-started">Choose your agent and see the commands ↓</a>
    </div>
    <MemoryCamp />
  </section>
  <nav class="page-sections section" aria-label="On this page"><a href="#product">See the app</a><a href="#get-started">Get started</a><a href="#how">How it works</a><a href="#features">What’s available</a><a href="#evidence">Evidence</a><a href="#questions">Questions</a></nav>
  <ProductTour />
  <section class="section" id="get-started"><div class="section-heading"><p class="eyebrow">Your first connected session</p><h2>Use Lore with the agent you already have.</h2><p>Choose your client, install Lore, then check that your first conversation appears in the local app.</p></div><AgentSetup id="home-setup" /></section>
  <section class="section" id="everyday"><div class="section-heading"><p class="eyebrow">In your everyday work</p><h2>Keep the reasoning that would otherwise need explaining again.</h2></div><dl class="capabilities"><div><dt>Continue yesterday’s bug fix</dt><dd>Search earlier conversations for the error, command, or rejected approach before starting over. <a href={`${base}docs/using-lore/#find-an-earlier-decision`}>Find a decision →</a></dd></div><div><dt>Switch coding agents</dt><dd>Keep project knowledge in <code>.lore.md</code> when you connect another supported agent to the same project. <a href={`${base}docs/using-lore/#switch-agents`}>Switch agents →</a></dd></div><div><dt>Share a project convention</dt><dd>Review a knowledge diff in a pull request so a teammate can start with the same rule. <a href={`${base}docs/team-memory/`}>Share through Git →</a></dd></div></dl></section>
  <section class="section" id="how"><span id="unified" class="fragment-anchor" aria-hidden="true"></span><div class="section-heading"><p class="eyebrow">The workflow</p><h2>From conversation to reusable knowledge.</h2><p>Recent work stays in context. Older work can be distilled, and details remain searchable.</p></div><ol class="workflow">
    <li><span>01</span><h3>Work in your agent</h3><p>Launch a supported agent through Lore. The local gateway records the conversation as you work.</p></li>
    <li><span>02</span><h3>Keep useful context</h3><p>Lore distills older conversation and curates decisions, patterns, and gotchas into project knowledge.</p></li>
    <li><span>03</span><h3>Inspect and reuse it</h3><p>Browse memory locally, review <code>.lore.md</code> changes, and let your agent search for relevant details.</p></li>
  </ol><a class="text-link" href={`${base}docs/memory/`}>See what is stored and what reaches the agent →</a></section>
  <section class="section availability" id="features"><span id="local-first" class="fragment-anchor" aria-hidden="true"></span><div class="section-heading"><p class="eyebrow">Available now</p><h2>Local memory, with a record you can review.</h2></div><dl class="capabilities">
    <div><dt>For your sessions</dt><dd>Conversation storage, context management, and recall through supported coding agents.</dd></div>
    <div><dt>For your project</dt><dd>Knowledge in an open SQLite database and a human-readable <code>.lore.md</code> file.</dd></div>
    <div><dt>For your team today</dt><dd>Commit <code>.lore.md</code> and review knowledge changes in pull requests. This shares knowledge through Git, rather than syncing conversation history.</dd></div>
  </dl><p><a href={`${base}docs/team-memory/`}>Read about Git-based sharing</a> · <a href="#waitlist">Interested in live team sync?</a></p></section>
  <section class="section evidence" id="evidence"><span id="voices" class="fragment-anchor" aria-hidden="true"></span><div class="section-heading"><p class="eyebrow">Published repository evaluation</p><h2>One long session, 20 questions.</h2><p>The repository reports recall results for a five-day coding session of approximately 2.3 million tokens, compared with a simulated compaction baseline.</p></div><div class="table-scroll"><table><caption>Historical results from the published mega-session evaluation</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Measure</th><th scope="col">Lore</th><th scope="col">Simulated compaction</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Average judged accuracy</th><td>4.0 / 5</td><td>2.4 / 5</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Perfect answers</th><td>13/20</td><td>5/20</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class="small muted">This is a single session and evaluation setup, not a guarantee for every project or model. These are published historical results, not a new run of the current release.</p><a class="text-link" href={`${base}docs/benchmark/`}>Read the methodology and limitations →</a><p><a href={`${base}docs/example/`}>Try a small project-memory example yourself →</a></p></section>
  <section class="section" id="questions"><div class="section-heading"><p class="eyebrow">Before you install</p><h2>A few practical questions.</h2></div><div class="faq">
    <details><summary>Do I need a different coding agent?</summary><p>No. Lore supports existing clients including Claude Code, Codex, OpenCode, Pi, and Hermes. Follow the guide for your client; desktop apps and terminal launchers have different setup paths. <a href={`${base}docs/install/`}>Choose your setup</a>.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Where does my data go?</summary><p>Memory is stored locally in SQLite and Markdown. Agent requests and Lore’s background LLM work use your configured providers. Production builds send privacy-filtered operational telemetry, which you can disable. <a href={`${base}docs/install/#operational-telemetry`}>Read the telemetry and storage details</a>.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Will a knowledge file appear after one message?</summary><p>Not necessarily. Distillation and curation run in the background and depend on available conversation and configuration. Check recorded sessions in the local browser first, then review any knowledge Lore extracts.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Does local Lore include live team sync?</summary><p>Git-based knowledge sharing is available today. Folk Lore’s hosted team experience is in early access; joining the list below expresses interest and does not enable sync.</p></details>
  </div></section>
  <Waitlist />
</PublicLayout>
